---
import '../../styles/property-table.css';
import Reference from '../../layouts/reference.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
const rows = [
  ['textures', 'Loaded textures or cache names. Use one for a static image, or at least two from the same sheet image for animation.', 'Built-in fallback', 'Texture objects\nCached names'],
  ['sizeMode', 'manual uses particle size. For animation, auto keeps the frame’s aspect ratio and applies particle size as a multiplier.', 'manual', 'manual\nauto'],
  ['pivot', 'Offset from the center. Positive X moves right; positive Y moves up.', '{ x: 0, y: 0 }', '{ x, y }'],
  ['flipX', 'Mirror the artwork horizontally.', 'false', 'true\nfalse'],
  ['flipY', 'Mirror the artwork vertically.', 'false', 'true\nfalse'],
  ['playback', 'Choose how animation frames play. Once modes span each particle’s lifetime; loop modes repeat at the chosen FPS.', 'loop-forward', 'none\nonce-forward\nonce-backward\nonce-pingpong\nloop-forward\nloop-backward\nloop-pingpong'],
  ['fps', 'Frames per second for looping animations.', '30', '0 or more'],
  ['animationSize', 'Reference dimensions for animation frames.', 'First frame size', '{ width, height }\nBoth greater than 0'],
];
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'load-a-texture', text: 'Load a texture' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'load-a-sprite-sheet', text: 'Load a sprite sheet' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'animate-particles', text: 'Animate particles' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'artwork-options', text: 'Artwork options' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'cleanup', text: 'Clean up' },
];
---
<Reference wide headings={headings} title="Textures & animation" description="Load images or sprite sheets with PixiJS, then choose which texture each emitter uses.">
  <h2 id="load-a-texture">Load a texture</h2>
  <p>Load your image with <code>Assets.load()</code> before creating the effect. Pass the texture under the emitter’s ID: <code>sparks</code> below matches <code>id: 'sparks'</code> in the effect definition.</p>
  <Code theme="github-dark-dimmed" code={`import { Assets, type Texture } from 'pixi.js';
import { ParticleEffect } from '@reparticle/pixi';

const spark = await Assets.load<Texture>('/textures/spark.png');

const effect = new ParticleEffect({
  version: 1,
  emitters: [{
    id: 'sparks', // Emitter ID.
    capacity: 2048,
    seed: 42,
    emission: { rate: 120, shape: { type: 'point' } },
    initial: { lifetime: 2, speed: 90, direction: 0, spread: Math.PI * 2, size: 12 },
  }],
}, {
  ticker: app.ticker,
  emitters: {
    sparks: { textures: [spark] }, // Must match the emitter ID above.
  },
});

effect.position.set(app.screen.width / 2, app.screen.height / 2);
app.stage.addChild(effect);`} lang="ts" />
  <p>The artwork key <code>sparks</code> must match the emitter’s <code>id</code> exactly. The texture variable <code>spark</code> and filename <code>spark.png</code> can have any name.</p>
  <p>This uses your existing PixiJS app. For multiple emitters, add an entry for each ID under <code>emitters</code>. An emitter without a supplied texture uses the built-in fallback.</p>

  <h2 id="load-a-sprite-sheet">Load a sprite sheet</h2>
  <p>A sprite sheet packs several images into one PNG. Its JSON file tells PixiJS where each image is.</p>
  <p>For example, put these exported files in your textures folder:</p>
  <Code theme="github-dark-dimmed" code={`textures/
  particles.png
  particles.json`} lang="text" />
  <p>Load <code>particles.json</code>. PixiJS also loads <code>particles.png</code>, as long as that is the image referenced in the JSON. Then choose a frame and give it to your emitter:</p>
  <Code theme="github-dark-dimmed" code={`import { Assets } from 'pixi.js';

await Assets.load('/textures/particles.json');

effect.setEmitterTexture('sparks', {
  textures: ['star.png'],
});`} lang="ts" />
  <p><code>star.png</code> is the name of an image inside the sheet, not a separate file to load. PixiJS caches these names when the sheet loads. A frame named <code>star</code> can be passed as <code>textures: ['star']</code>. Replace it with one of the names listed under <code>frames</code> in your JSON.</p>
  <p><code>sparks</code> selects the emitter from the previous example. This gives all its particles the star texture; it does not animate them.</p>

  <h2 id="animate-particles">Animate particles</h2>
  <p>After loading the sprite sheet, list its frame names in playback order. Use at least two frames from the same sheet image:</p>
  <Code theme="github-dark-dimmed" code={`effect.setEmitterTexture('sparks', {
  textures: [
    'fire-01.png',
    'fire-02.png',
    'fire-03.png',
  ],
  playback: 'loop-forward',
  fps: 12,
});`} lang="ts" />
  <p>You can also pass these same options under <code>emitters.sparks</code> when creating <code>ParticleEffect</code>. Use <code>loop-forward</code> to repeat at the chosen FPS, or <code>once-forward</code> to play once over each particle’s lifetime.</p>

  <h2 id="artwork-options">Artwork options</h2>
  <div class="property-table">
    <table aria-label="Artwork settings">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Description</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Required</th><th scope="col">Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(([name, description, defaultValue, value]) => <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>{name}</code></th>
          <td class="property-description">{description}</td>
          <td>{defaultValue}</td>
          <td>No</td>
          <td class="property-range">{value}</td>
        </tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cleanup">Clean up</h2>
  <p>Destroy the effect when removing it from your scene. Loaded textures remain available for other effects. Unload an asset only when nothing else uses it:</p>
  <Code theme="github-dark-dimmed" code={`effect.destroy();

// Only when these assets are no longer used by your scene:
await Assets.unload('/textures/spark.png');
await Assets.unload('/textures/particles.json');`} lang="ts" />
</Reference>
